[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defence"]
[Variation "2.Nf3, Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "B13"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "fork"]
[Themes "transposition,solid-centre,queenside-space"]
[For "black"]
[Kind "main"]
[Opponent "2.Nf3"]
[Start "short-system 1...c6"]

2. Nf3 { The most popular second move at club level. White develops first and keeps the choice between d4, d3 and Nc3 for later. } 2... d5 { The same answer as against 2.d4: hit e4 with a pawn that the c6 pawn supports. } 3. exd5 { Swaps the e-pawn so the centre opens and White does not have to defend e4. } ( 3. Nc3 { The same position as 2.Nc3 d5 3.Nf3, the Two Knights Attack. Black pins the knight with ...Bg4. } ) ( 3. d3 { The same position as 2.d3 d5 3.Nf3. Black pins the knight with ...Bg4. } ) ( 3. d4 { Leaves e4 with no defender, because the knight on f3 has taken the f3 square from the f-pawn. } 3... dxe4 { Takes the pawn. White cannot win it back quickly. } 4. Ng5 ( 4. Ne5 Nf6 ) 4... Nf6 { Guards the extra pawn on e4 and develops. Black follows with ...e6 and ...Bb4 or ...Be7, a pawn up. } ) 3... cxd5 { Recaptures towards the centre. The c-file is now half open for Black's rooks. } 4. d4 { The usual move. The game is now the Exchange Variation, the same position as 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Nf3. } ( 4. Bb5+ Nc6 { Blocks the check with a piece that wants to come out anyway. After d4 the game is back in the main line. } 5. d4 ) ( 4. Nc3 Nc6 { Develops and keeps ...Bg4 in hand. After d4 the game is the line with 5.Nc3. } 5. d4 ) ( 4. c4 Nf6 { Develops and guards d5 a second time. After d4 the game is the Panov Attack. } 5. d4 ( 5. cxd5 { The same position as 2.c4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.cxd5 Nf6 5.Nf3: Black takes back on d5 with the knight. } ) ) ( 4. Be2 Nc6 5. d4 ) ( 4. c3 Nc6 5. d4 ) ( 4. d3 { The same position as 2.d3 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Nf3. Black plays ...Nc6 and ...e5, taking the centre White has left alone. } ) 4... Nc6 { Develops and puts pressure on d4. It also covers e5, so White's knight cannot settle there. } 5. Bb5 { Pins the knight to the king, so for now it does not hit d4 or guard e5. } ( 5. c3 Bg4 { Pins the knight on f3, which guards d4. Black keeps the same plan: ...e6, ...Bd6 and ...Nge7 or ...Nf6. } 6. Be2 ( 6. Qb3 Qd7 { Guards b7 and d5 at once, so the queen on b3 hits nothing. } ) 6... e6 ) ( 5. Bd3 { The same position as 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Bd3 Nc6 5.Nf3. Black pins the knight with ...Bg4. } ) ( 5. c4 Nf6 { Develops and guards d5. } 6. Nc3 e6 { Supports d5 and opens the f8 bishop. If White takes on d5, Black takes back and plays against the pawn on d4, which has no neighbours. } ) ( 5. Be2 Bg4 { Pins the knight that guards d4, as in the other lines. } 6. O-O e6 ) 5... e6 { Supports d5 and opens the way for the f8 bishop, so Black can castle soon. } 6. O-O ( 6. Ne5 Bd7 { Guards c6 a second time. White was threatening Nxc6, and after ...bxc6 the check Bxc6+ would fork the king and the rook on a8. } 7. Nxd7 Qxd7 { Black has given up the bishop but is quicker to develop, and the queen covers c6 and d5. } ) ( 6. Nc3 Bd6 7. O-O { The same position as the main line. } ) ( 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Black keeps both bishops, the pawn on c6 guards d5, and the b-file opens for a rook. ...Bd6, ...Ne7 and ...O-O follow. } ) 6... Bd6 { An active square: the bishop aims at h2 and lets Black castle next. } 7. Nc3 ( 7. Re1 Nge7 ) ( 7. c3 Nge7 ) ( 7. Bg5 Nge7 { Blocks the bishop's attack on the queen and gets ready to castle. } ) ( 7. c4 Nge7 ) 7... Nge7 { The knight goes to e7 rather than f6, so Bg5 cannot pin it and the f-pawn stays free. From e7 it can go to f5 or g6. } 8. Re1 O-O { Black is castled with a solid centre and no weak pawns. Plan: ...a6 and ...b5 to gain room on the queenside, a knight to f5 to press on d4, and a rook to c8 on the open file. } *
